  "references": [
    "Feldman EC, Nelson RW. Periparturient TREATMENT •Outpatient for healthy bitch or queen. •Instruct owner to monitor rectal",
    "Grundy SG, Davidson AP. Theriogenology temperature and observe for signs of systemic illness. •Ovariohysterectomy—curative; recommended if future breeding is not a consideration."
  ],
  "credits": {
    "author": "Joni L. Freshman •Surgical removal—indicated if medical",
    "consulting_editor": "Erin E. Runcan treatment is unsuccessful and the bitch develops metritis. MEDICATIONS DRUG(S) OF CHOICE •Oxytocin—known or suspected condition in otherwise healthy cats and dogs; dogs: 0.5 IU/kg IM, up to 5 IU total dose; cats: 0.5–1 IU IM. Oxytocin may be ineffective after 48 hours postpartum. •Can precede oxytocin treatment with calcium gluconate (10%); dogs and cats, 0.5–1.5 mL/kg IV slowly over 15 minutes; monitor for bradycardia during injection. •Metritis—treat accordingly (see Metritis). CONTRAINDICATIONS/POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS Do not give progestational drugs. FOLLOW-UP •Monitor temperature and physical condition. •Acute metritis (dogs)—may develop if the placenta is not passed; fair to good prognosis for recovery with treatment. •Prognosis for future reproduction—good without metritis; fair to poor with metritis. MISCELLANEOUS diseases. In: Feldman EC, Nelson RW, eds., Canine and Feline Endocrinology and Reproduction, 3rd ed. Philadelphia, PA: Saunders, 2004, pp. 808–834. question of the month. Acute metritis secondary to retained fetal membranes and a retained nonviable fetus. J Am Vet Med Assoc 2004, 224(6):844–847.",
    "acknowledgment": null
